    <section class="row">
      <div class="label">The building</div>
      <div class="body">
        <p>The building at 6121 Havelock Avenue was built in 1910 by brothers Volney and Roy Headrick and began as a saloon, where a third brother, Oney, tended bar. Two years later it was repainted and refitted as the Joyo Theatre, opening with picture reels and comedy acrobats for five or ten cents. Havelock was then an independent railroad town, incorporated in 1893 around the Burlington Railroad&rsquo;s locomotive and rail-car shops, where engines and cars were built and repaired. It grew as a working-class community of the families who depended on them, until Lincoln annexed it in 1930.</p>
        <p>Over the decades the building housed many other businesses. In 1937 the Joyo name moved across the avenue to the Lyric Theatre, which still operates today. Officina Marlo occupies the original theater building.</p>
      </div>
